== Re-bind tool, fixed after the owner's amendment (before the re-bind ran) ==
rebind_evaluation.py:
  inherited_changes() reads the previous package's evaluation-comparison.json, only after checking its sha256 against
  the previous evaluation manifest's outputs, and takes its cumulative changes (B5's package: its declarations).
  compose() appends this re-bind's declarations to that chain. A declaration on an inherited gene and field must
  continue it exactly (start at its `to`, same rows); a change reverted to its `from` disappears; anything else stops.
  Asserts, against the historical benchmark, field counts and exact (gene, field, from, to) -> rows equal to the
  cumulative chain; against the previous package, equal to this re-bind's own declarations; the same split for
  discrepancy detail. The comparison records inherited, declared and cumulative changes; the manifest summarises the
  cumulative ones; the report says which comparison shows which change.
scripts/compatible_bundle.py comparison_supports_result: the historical difference must equal the cumulative counts,
  the difference from the previous evaluation must equal this re-bind's declared counts, and every declaration must
  appear in the chain. A comparison without the cumulative field (B5's) is its own chain.
Tests (test_compatible_bundle.py, 5 new): an inherited change supports a re-bind that declares nothing; a historical
  difference missing from the chain is refused; an inherited change reappearing against the previous evaluation is
  refused; a declaration outside the chain is refused; B5's published comparison still verifies. The helper now
  defaults the difference-from-previous to the historical one (B5's case), so the six B5 tests keep their meaning.
compose() controls, run once (the audit script is not in CI): appends a new gene/field, continues a chain
  (from x to z, both reasons kept), drops a reverted change, refuses a non-continuation and a row-count mismatch,
  leaves the inherited list untouched. All as expected.
SB6-g bundle rule ignores the difference from the previous evaluation: 1 red (the reappearing-change test).
SB6-h bundle rule stops requiring declarations in the chain: 1 red (the outside-the-chain test). Restored identical.

